#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259707 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{3}$ traversed from the point $P(1,1)$ to point $Q(2,8),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259708 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(x+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(z-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,z=2t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(2t+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2t)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\mathbf{i}+6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((2t+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (2t\\mathbf{i}+6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+12{t}^{2}-18{t}^{3}+8{t}^{2} \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}-16{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{t}^{2}-16{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259710 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sec}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sec}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}t)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{sec}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sec}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{sec}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{tan}t-\\operatorname{tan}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2t)}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2t)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(0)}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259711 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+(\\operatorname{sin}t+1)\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259712 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that  $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{cos}t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{cos}t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(-1-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259713 :question/problem "Evaluate$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t(-\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259714 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)={e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,z={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-1+1+2{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{4t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259715 :question/problem "Evaluate  $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ((\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)+t\\operatorname{cos}t(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =t({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259716 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[1-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259717 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t},\\,y=3{e}^{2t},\\,z={e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{2t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\mathbf{i}+6{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (2{e}^{t}\\mathbf{i}+6{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}+6-6 \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259718 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{y+1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [3,4].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{y+1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-1)+1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2({t}^{2}-1)}{t-1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot 2t-2(t+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-2(t+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =[-16+9] \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(2xz+\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{i}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{j}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2xy{z}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) & =3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259730 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=-x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259731 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$  is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,xy\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected.  The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the coordinate axes.  Thus, the region is formed by four disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259803 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dca985dc352c45477dbb6ada86ad5a9cfa4404969510191a1955e59b082709fe.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/eb/eb4da8143700c7d31e0ceacd09ac76a8d1bcb10379a34dce5b59d48f2ae58c13.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/70a473397216589350f63482ab3e36cb1247e7825046c70642254c8514564b00.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259804 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259805 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259806 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}3t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259807 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y+2z\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=-y,R=2z.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259808 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t)+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259809 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-\\sqrt{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\sqrt{3})\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t)+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259811 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)+\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259812 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{5}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{5}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{5}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =5+5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =10(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =20\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da7dc42ad4e9eb5b6df7c42f06d7a0f7767820693f8e99e073c7b8bd7e93441.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/14/14e94a2c5d0faabff1beeadeccb6999aa2edd71fce06fff8a2655db74e585eba.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c54487081f2150216b7e41b1351f0f7b19c794b140297789f19d9afe830bd7b1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259814 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0daaf51b1aab267c11b8457b0e7e1480ff5aabd3acaa15a07f54625c6d8103d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/17/1764bfc51a25475162a70bad7239a36f5d099aa6157c71ed7ed3e02e474eb906.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/05/059731f0cac61f19382172192c6a9f7f366ecb758767797f9bc8311faa3865af.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259815 :question/problem "![](images/61/61147f12b8c73562b7153740066ef8c7209d3dc84612d3667630bc9460918c03.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a6517f5b42ed2ffd18522ce13d817c3094245c650c41d0f456116914c818436.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/38/38868b69ae1836844a279e141d83b886f386c87edeedbb1e2067ffd18d7f1af3.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259816 :question/problem "![](images/99/99eab39ead09005a6b877e540e4330e2e29a21230b48439ac779f89953c69860.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/c0/c075bf6fa60af07e70569395b257d25bb53f5edab8ff14f4a772271c9df7e153.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/41/41528079b285fb3ba20fcac0bd28f42ed7981a775938286654cf7319c1e8a6c6.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259817 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8dc3102ab0055d4e7dde7c79873a2c3d825fa9c803ea1ee2376a7dbe920c5535.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the quarter circle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/ac/ac9d8388e73a33df98ac7f04fe161ae75b27b4b01a79213c07e10d43c328d747.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/92/92e6f3ca98d7adbb34765307c0c570f55ce85f7f10cf6bef28bdb93d68a0c1cb.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259818 :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee674d6140d9326894d607ca9a3feede580ce68bd56ca762a013a9602a657832.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d6/d69e4c3dee5a346baae0accffc4d234b5b84e0402d107b5d1a24160a796992dd.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/61/61a1ebee96c7082485b318d5bcc1f55a42c0f4589b0aad9edf09e4deb82a2403.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259819 :question/problem "![](images/97/97f1287fa4c3662c070914e4cc93879cebaa72b07e2b2b5c03143941c647ed11.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the quarter circle traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/e0/e07fca985a11d4edb66ea91d9a560ab134d81f5f20aaba8b376677d0de2dd3e7.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/78/78de6ce0133bec33c14eabb43db2e53eb8d76653aa1fc121c83f47c4b91c3b94.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259820 :question/problem "![](images/86/866a1812bd31e0443becf35a7cc2b8df1bc84a49091a94250b60fe3533e4b172.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4232577b0687d781f702a196ae2c0060d3264c1f44a45a404a0f9da03bc0dd1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7cfcef37ed6a52d5bd374fae2d7a00c4d8012e9ea8dbef43165c10bcc9471be.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259828 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259829 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259830 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x+5\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-5x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x+5)\\cdot {e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x+5)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5x}}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259831 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+7\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+7)\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+7)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259834 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e60f7c0adc3e2ec8daa3203564c31bf660e6eca7e6394955cc73133ac3673ed5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a8010db8481768cb61d0734dcfc97eed646441ab265c4928d06d274ef0e1c1dd.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/be/be263d706bfc3ef205be37aaed85d4984d3739d53cdaadb8cba88e3d5196ce93.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259835 :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4042d4eb43310480f3c75673befbf58067a89704d20b9a63f0f6c7272179f3e.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ are false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/fa/fabbbb78d8fd4d327fe82d1d93d8e0fc15dd33e909266e0ef88de931be28b951.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/71/712564e3b0ad0e23d2d9097eb02c0857130bdaa6619a005738205130ab66ac3a.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259836 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$∇f$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∇f$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."}]}
